1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,054
Good evening, and welcome.

2
00:00:02,054 --> 00:00:09,400
The lights are low, and the shelves around you hold thousands of dark stones from the deserts of southern Peru.

3
00:00:09,866 --> 00:00:11,635
Look closely at this one.

4
00:00:11,635 --> 00:00:13,263
A long-necked dinosaur.

5
00:00:13,263 --> 00:00:16,164
And beside it, a human being, hunting it.

6
00:00:16,164 --> 00:00:19,278
On another shelf, men perform heart surgery.

7
00:00:19,278 --> 00:00:23,100
On another, a man studies the sky through a telescope.

8
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:34,966
The man who built this collection believed these stones were a library left by a lost human race, older than the dinosaurs' extinction, who finally departed for the stars.

9
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:37,486
Tonight I am your audio guide.

10
00:00:37,486 --> 00:00:40,977
We will walk through the museum one room at a time.

11
00:00:40,977 --> 00:00:47,000
In each room, we hear what the stones are said to show, and then what the evidence says.

12
00:00:47,466 --> 00:00:48,290
Seven rooms.

13
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:52,343
One exit, where we sign the guestbook and give our verdict.

14
00:00:52,343 --> 00:00:54,266
Press play, and step inside.

15
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:00,879
Room one: the birthday stone.

16
00:01:00,879 --> 00:01:10,500
Our story begins in Ica, a desert town about three hundred kilometres south of Lima, on the thirteenth of May, nineteen sixty-six.

17
00:01:10,966 --> 00:01:17,486
It is the forty-second birthday of a respected physician, Dr Javier Cabrera Darquea.

18
00:01:17,486 --> 00:01:26,566
His friend, the photographer Félix Llosa Romero, hands him a present: a small stone with a picture scratched into it.

19
00:01:27,033 --> 00:01:32,908
Cabrera decides the picture shows a fish that has been extinct for millions of years.

20
00:01:32,908 --> 00:01:33,668
Which fish?

21
00:01:33,668 --> 00:01:34,635
He never said.

22
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:35,465
Why extinct?

23
00:01:35,465 --> 00:01:36,778
He never explained.

24
00:01:36,778 --> 00:01:39,266
But from that moment, he was hooked.

25
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:44,873
Two brothers, Carlos and Pablo Soldi, had thousands of similar stones.

26
00:01:44,873 --> 00:01:52,509
They said a flood of the Ica River in nineteen sixty-one had washed them out of the ground near Ocucaje.

27
00:01:52,509 --> 00:01:55,666
Cabrera bought three hundred and forty-one.

28
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:59,938
Then he found a supplier who could bring him far more.

29
00:01:59,938 --> 00:02:02,122
A farmer named Basilio Uschuya.

30
00:02:02,122 --> 00:02:06,491
Over the following years, the stones arrived by the thousands.

31
00:02:06,491 --> 00:02:07,830
Remember that name.

32
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:10,366
We will meet him again in room five.

33
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:15,675
Meanwhile, something happened that the legend rarely mentions.

34
00:02:15,675 --> 00:02:29,966
In nineteen sixty-six, the architect Santiago Agurto Calvo and the Ica museum curator Alejandro Pezzia dug in ancient cemeteries, and reported small engraved stones inside real tombs.

35
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:32,462
What did those stones show?

36
00:02:32,462 --> 00:02:33,139
A flower.

37
00:02:33,139 --> 00:02:33,665
A fish.

38
00:02:33,665 --> 00:02:34,266
A llama.

39
00:02:34,266 --> 00:02:35,244
No dinosaurs.

40
00:02:35,244 --> 00:02:36,146
No surgeons.

41
00:02:36,146 --> 00:02:38,175
Hold on to that difference.

42
00:02:38,175 --> 00:02:41,633
It is the most important clue in the building.

43
00:02:42,099 --> 00:02:43,857
Room two: the dinosaurs.

44
00:02:43,857 --> 00:02:47,153
This is the room that made the museum famous.

45
00:02:47,153 --> 00:02:52,133
Long-necked giants, horned beasts, plated backs and flying reptiles.

46
00:02:52,599 --> 00:02:56,894
People are shown hunting them, fighting them, even riding them.

47
00:02:56,894 --> 00:03:04,666
Fans point to one scene where hunters strike at a precise spot on a dinosaur's spine, as if they knew its anatomy.

48
00:03:05,133 --> 00:03:09,416
If these were made by eyewitnesses, it would rewrite everything.

49
00:03:09,416 --> 00:03:11,423
Humans and dinosaurs together.

50
00:03:11,423 --> 00:03:13,966
Young-earth creationists still say so.

51
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:15,712
Now the evidence.

52
00:03:15,712 --> 00:03:21,130
The last non-avian dinosaurs died out about sixty-six million years ago.

53
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:29,633
No dinosaur fossil has ever been dated to the age of humans, and no dinosaur remains are known around Ica at all.

54
00:03:30,099 --> 00:03:32,242
And look at how they are drawn.

55
00:03:32,242 --> 00:03:36,873
The big meat-eater stands upright, its tail dragging on the ground.

56
00:03:36,873 --> 00:03:42,333
That is exactly how museums and films showed dinosaurs in the nineteen sixties.

57
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,280
Real eyewitnesses would have drawn the real animal.

58
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:51,400
These stones drew the textbook of their own decade, including its mistakes.

59
00:03:51,866 --> 00:03:57,341
Some meat-eaters have five fingers and five toes, which no fossil supports.

60
00:03:57,341 --> 00:04:02,670
Some stones show horned dinosaurs of a kind never found in South America.

61
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:08,000
Others show dinosaurs hatching as larvae and changing shape like insects.

62
00:04:08,466 --> 00:04:12,426
And here, a man rides a flying reptile like a horse.

63
00:04:12,426 --> 00:04:16,233
A real Pteranodon could never have lifted a human.

64
00:04:16,699 --> 00:04:17,902
One more detail.

65
00:04:17,902 --> 00:04:25,416
The stones only show prehistoric animals that scientists already knew about in the nineteen sixties.

66
00:04:25,416 --> 00:04:27,294
Not a single new species.

67
00:04:27,294 --> 00:04:30,000
The library, it seems, was borrowed.

68
00:04:30,466 --> 00:04:32,411
Room three: the surgeons.

69
00:04:32,411 --> 00:04:38,166
Cabrera was a doctor, and these were the stones he showed the world first.

70
00:04:38,166 --> 00:04:39,566
Heart transplants.

71
00:04:39,566 --> 00:04:40,888
Brain operations.

72
00:04:40,888 --> 00:04:42,211
Caesarean births.

73
00:04:42,211 --> 00:04:44,466
Kidney and liver transplants.

74
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:49,133
Supporters say only a culture with modern medicine could have drawn this.

75
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,328
But look at the surgeons.

76
00:04:51,328 --> 00:04:55,339
Big heads, long noses, loincloths and feather headdresses.

77
00:04:55,339 --> 00:04:58,728
In battle scenes they fight with axes and spears.

78
00:04:58,728 --> 00:05:03,500
A civilization that transplants hearts, but still fights with spears?

79
00:05:03,966 --> 00:05:05,553
Now notice the timing.

80
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:12,840
The first human heart transplant made world headlines in December nineteen sixty-seven, in Cape Town.

81
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:17,024
The surgery stones became famous in the years right after.

82
00:05:17,024 --> 00:05:19,333
On its own, that proves nothing.

83
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:30,433
And there is a stranger detail, which we will hear in room five: one maker later said he engraved stones from drawings that Cabrera himself brought to him.

84
00:05:30,899 --> 00:05:35,257
Skeptics also note how open these pictures are to reading.

85
00:05:35,257 --> 00:05:40,066
An open chest could be mutilation or a ritual, not a transplant.

86
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:48,042
Surgery stones, then, tell us a lot about the world of the nineteen sixties, and about the man who collected them.

87
00:05:48,042 --> 00:05:51,533
About ancient Peru, so far, they tell us very little.

88
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,326
Room four: the sky.

89
00:05:53,326 --> 00:05:55,072
Here are the astronomers.

90
00:05:55,072 --> 00:05:58,703
Men with what look like telescopes, watching comets.

91
00:05:58,703 --> 00:06:00,100
And flying machines.

92
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:10,737
One ancient mysteries author, David Hatcher Childress, joked that the telescope stone could just as easily show a game of prehistoric tennis.

93
00:06:10,737 --> 00:06:14,633
And many of the flying machines look a lot like birds.

94
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:16,891
Cabrera went much further.

95
00:06:16,891 --> 00:06:30,533
He wrote that the stones were made by Gliptolithic Man: humans with bigger brains, who used psychic energy to influence the heavens, and who existed at least four hundred and five million years ago.

96
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:41,964
Before a great comet struck, he said, they left Earth for a planet in the Pleiades, launching their craft from the plains of Nazca, which he called an ancient spaceport.

97
00:06:41,964 --> 00:06:45,533
And they had built the pyramids of Egypt along the way.

98
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:56,578
Ancient astronaut writers of the nineteen seventies, Erich von Däniken and Robert Charroux among them, carried the stones to a worldwide audience.

99
00:06:56,578 --> 00:07:01,433
The Nazca desert has its own disputed bodies, a separate case file.

100
00:07:01,899 --> 00:07:07,708
But even among fringe writers, Cabrera's cosmic history found few followers.

101
00:07:07,708 --> 00:07:14,433
Over time, von Däniken himself concluded that many of the stones were most likely fakes.

102
00:07:14,899 --> 00:07:17,645
Which brings us to the obvious question.

103
00:07:17,645 --> 00:07:21,558
If the stones were not made by sky people, who made them?

104
00:07:21,558 --> 00:07:25,333
For that, we have to leave the museum and visit a farm.

105
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:27,729
Room five: the workshop.

106
00:07:27,729 --> 00:07:30,785
This room is not in the museum at all.

107
00:07:30,785 --> 00:07:39,066
It is a farmhouse in Callango, in the Ica valley, home of Basilio Uschuya and his wife, Irma Gutiérrez.

108
00:07:39,533 --> 00:07:47,080
In January nineteen seventy-five, a Peruvian magazine called Mundial published an interview with the couple.

109
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:52,530
Every stone they had sold to Cabrera, they said, they had engraved themselves.

110
00:07:52,530 --> 00:07:56,933
The pictures came from comic books, school books and magazines.

111
00:07:57,399 --> 00:08:01,789
Their tools were simple: knives, chisels, and a dentist's drill.

112
00:08:01,789 --> 00:08:05,286
The designs are not carved deep into the hard rock.

113
00:08:05,286 --> 00:08:09,333
They are scratched through a thin dark skin on the surface.

114
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:10,774
Then the aging.

115
00:08:10,774 --> 00:08:17,600
The fresh stones were baked in cow and donkey dung, and rubbed with boot polish, to darken the scratches.

116
00:08:18,066 --> 00:08:21,556
Another time, Basilio described an easier recipe.

117
00:08:21,556 --> 00:08:27,466
Put the engraved stones in the chicken pen, he said, and the chickens did the rest.

118
00:08:27,933 --> 00:08:32,201
In nineteen seventy-seven a BBC film crew came to Ica.

119
00:08:32,201 --> 00:08:40,500
Producer Ray Sutcliffe described Basilio as intelligent, proud of his work, with a great sense of humour.

120
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:48,215
For the camera, he took a fresh stone, switched on his drill, and engraved an ancient-looking picture in minutes.

121
00:08:48,215 --> 00:08:51,166
A simple stone can take about fifteen minutes.

122
00:08:51,633 --> 00:08:52,356
Why do it?

123
00:08:52,356 --> 00:08:54,815
His answer was disarmingly honest.

124
00:08:54,815 --> 00:08:59,299
Making these stones, he said, is easier than farming the land.

125
00:08:59,766 --> 00:09:01,066
There was a catch.

126
00:09:01,066 --> 00:09:04,533
Selling genuine Peruvian antiquities is illegal.

127
00:09:04,533 --> 00:09:08,361
After the BBC film, the authorities arrested Basilio.

128
00:09:08,361 --> 00:09:12,911
Under questioning he repeated it: the stones were his own work.

129
00:09:12,911 --> 00:09:17,100
He was released, and kept selling them, openly, as crafts.

130
00:09:17,566 --> 00:09:19,042
He was not alone.

131
00:09:19,042 --> 00:09:27,722
Other local makers, including Pedro Huamán and Aparicio Aparcana, also admitted to engraving stones.

132
00:09:27,722 --> 00:09:30,066
Ica had a cottage industry.

133
00:09:30,533 --> 00:09:32,310
Room six: the laboratory.

134
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:39,704
Believers answered the confession with one strong argument: the stones are covered in an ancient patina.

135
00:09:39,704 --> 00:09:41,766
Surely that proves their age?

136
00:09:42,233 --> 00:09:43,424
Here is the key.

137
00:09:43,424 --> 00:09:45,879
The stone itself can be very old.

138
00:09:45,879 --> 00:09:49,749
Andesite weathers slowly, forming a thin, soft rind.

139
00:09:49,749 --> 00:09:55,033
But the age of a stone is not the age of the picture scratched into it.

140
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:06,419
When the BBC's stone was examined in London, the rock looked ancient, but the edges of the engraving were clean and sharp, cut after the surface had weathered.

141
00:10:06,419 --> 00:10:12,600
Investigator Neil Steede saw the same thing: patina on the stone, none inside the grooves.

142
00:10:13,066 --> 00:10:21,833
In the early nineteen nineties, stones examined in Barcelona showed traces of modern work: sandpaper, saws and acid.

143
00:10:22,299 --> 00:10:24,587
What about Cabrera's own tests?

144
00:10:24,587 --> 00:10:30,415
He said a professor in Bonn and a mining engineer friend had confirmed the age.

145
00:10:30,415 --> 00:10:34,399
No laboratory report from Bonn has ever been produced.

146
00:10:34,866 --> 00:10:36,295
Then came the clay.

147
00:10:36,295 --> 00:10:46,145
In nineteen ninety-seven, a German TV crew filmed a back room full of clay figurines: dinosaurs hatching from eggs, even kangaroos.

148
00:10:46,145 --> 00:10:48,776
Basilio said he had made those too.

149
00:10:48,776 --> 00:10:51,333
Clay, unlike stone, can be tested.

150
00:10:51,799 --> 00:10:58,027
Von Däniken broke an arm off one figurine and sent it to a laboratory in Zurich.

151
00:10:58,027 --> 00:10:59,506
The answer: modern.

152
00:10:59,506 --> 00:11:05,266
Samples sent to Weimar came back relatively young, still containing water.

153
00:11:05,733 --> 00:11:13,166
Supporters cite a two thousand three test dating a mineral crust on some stones to tens of thousands of years.

154
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:18,166
It appeared in a believers' book, and it dates the crust, not the drawing.

155
00:11:18,633 --> 00:11:20,562
Room seven: the locked door.

156
00:11:20,562 --> 00:11:23,111
Cabrera had an answer for everything.

157
00:11:23,111 --> 00:11:26,901
The farmer's stones, he said, were copies for tourists.

158
00:11:26,901 --> 00:11:30,966
Only twenty to forty fakes had slipped into his collection.

159
00:11:31,433 --> 00:11:41,137
The real originals, he said, came from a secret cave near the Ica River, guarded by local people, holding a hoard of untouched stones.

160
00:11:41,137 --> 00:11:43,600
Nobody else was allowed to see it.

161
00:11:44,066 --> 00:11:49,436
Investigator Neil Steede even offered to be blindfolded for the journey.

162
00:11:49,436 --> 00:11:53,388
He was refused, and concluded the cave never existed.

163
00:11:53,388 --> 00:11:57,266
No map, no photograph, no witness has ever surfaced.

164
00:11:57,733 --> 00:12:02,591
Von Däniken once wrote that Cabrera had taken him to the cave.

165
00:12:02,591 --> 00:12:08,233
When Cabrera disputed it, von Däniken admitted he had made that part up.

166
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:11,754
And Basilio himself kept the mystery alive.

167
00:12:11,754 --> 00:12:23,335
In nineteen ninety-five he told Steede that he had engraved stones from Cabrera's drawings, but that Cabrera also owned about five thousand stones he had not made.

168
00:12:23,335 --> 00:12:28,166
Later he even claimed his confession was only to stay out of prison.

169
00:12:28,633 --> 00:12:30,864
So could any stones be genuine?

170
00:12:30,864 --> 00:12:38,851
Possibly a few, like the small ones from real tombs, with flowers, fish, birds and llamas, finer and shallower.

171
00:12:38,851 --> 00:12:44,895
A Spanish report from the sixteenth century is also said to mention engraved stones.

172
00:12:44,895 --> 00:12:46,766
But none shows a dinosaur.

173
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:49,215
And here is the honest gap.

174
00:12:49,215 --> 00:12:56,189
No one has ever done an independent study to separate possibly ancient stones from modern ones.

175
00:12:56,189 --> 00:12:59,933
Behind this door, a real question is still waiting.

176
00:13:00,399 --> 00:13:01,418
Our tour is over.

177
00:13:01,418 --> 00:13:03,933
On the way out, let us sign the guestbook.

178
00:13:04,399 --> 00:13:08,483
Andesite pebbles with a soft weathered skin: evidence.

179
00:13:08,483 --> 00:13:12,718
Makers who confessed and demonstrated on film: evidence.

180
00:13:12,718 --> 00:13:17,407
Dinosaurs drawn like nineteen sixties museum models: evidence.

181
00:13:17,407 --> 00:13:21,566
A few plain motifs that may be truly ancient: possible.

182
00:13:22,033 --> 00:13:25,561
Humans living alongside dinosaurs: debunked.

183
00:13:25,561 --> 00:13:29,569
Gliptolithic Man and alien knowledge: no evidence.

184
00:13:29,569 --> 00:13:34,299
Exactly who engraved all fifty thousand stones: still open.

185
00:13:34,766 --> 00:13:35,693
The verdict.

186
00:13:35,693 --> 00:13:50,983
The famous dinosaur, surgery and telescope stones are modern engravings, made for sale from the nineteen sixties onward by farmers of the Ica valley, and read by a sincere doctor into a cosmic myth.

187
00:13:50,983 --> 00:13:51,987
No lost race.

188
00:13:51,987 --> 00:13:53,299
No alien library.

189
00:13:53,766 --> 00:14:03,333
What stays open is smaller and more human: which plain stones might be truly ancient, and how much the collector shaped the pictures he was sold.

190
00:14:03,799 --> 00:14:08,382
Cabrera called his stones the most important legacy of our time.

191
00:14:08,382 --> 00:14:10,029
In a way, he was right.

192
00:14:10,029 --> 00:14:19,766
They are a perfect record of the nineteen sixties: its dinosaurs, its heart surgeons, its space age dreams, scratched into ancient rock.

193
00:14:20,233 --> 00:14:21,721
Thank you for visiting.

194
00:14:21,721 --> 00:14:23,598
Which room changed your mind?

195
00:14:23,598 --> 00:14:29,033
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
